  "Question": "What is the sum of the zeros of \\(f(x)=x^2-5x+6\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "5",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The zeros are 2 and 3, and their sum is 5.\n\nAlternative method 1: Compare coefficients\n\nThe question asks only for the sum, so compare coefficients without finding each zero separately. The discriminant is \\(25-4\\cdot6=1>0\\), so there are two real zeros, say \\(r\\) and \\(s\\). Since the leading coefficient is 1,\n\\[\nx^2-5x+6=(x-r)(x-s)=x^2-(r+s)x+rs.\n\\]\nComparing the coefficients of \\(x\\) gives \\(-(r+s)=-5\\), so the sum of the zeros is \\(5\\).",
